{"fawn":{"id":"7425b92f-9cc9-4b2d-be9b-c08d06350fa0","word":"fawn","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to exhibit affection","word":"agradar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to seek favour by flattery","word":"puxar saco"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to seek favour by flattery","word":"bajular"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to seek favour by flattery","word":"adular"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["devotion","devotion"],["submissiveness","submissiveness"],["wag","wag"],["tail","tail"],["nuzzling","nuzzle"],["lick","lick"]],"raw_glosses":["(literal, of a dog) To show devotion or submissiveness by wagging its tail, nuzzling, licking, etc."],"qualifier":"literal","glosses":["To show devotion or submissiveness by wagging its tail, nuzzling, licking, etc."],"tags":["intransitive"],"raw_tags":["of a dog"],"id":"a0660e2f-d289-4de6-a89c-f5b21017eb1e"},{"raw_glosses":["(figurative, by extension)","To exhibit affection toward someone."],"links":[["exhibit","exhibit"],["affection","affection"]],"glosses":["To exhibit affection toward someone."],"tags":["broadly","figuratively","intransitive"],"id":"bd625220-b400-4cd6-9c62-c73eccd03b91"},{"raw_glosses":["(figurative, by extension)","To attempt to please someone."],"links":[["attempt","attempt"],["please","please"]],"glosses":["To attempt to please someone."],"tags":["broadly","figuratively","intransitive"],"id":"b51b7ef7-05d8-426c-88ed-3ff71556eabb"},{"examples":[{"text":"You showed your teeth like apes, and fawned like hounds.","ref":"1599 (first performance), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Iulius Cæsar”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act V, scene i]:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[37,43]],"id":"d7c8eede-e1f6-443d-9fa9-c9d8932c137a"},{"text":"Thou with trembling fear, / Or like a fawning parasite, obeyest.","ref":"1671, John Milton, Paradise Regained, book 1:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[38,45]],"id":"48323a72-5e4e-4cc4-ba63-c3c4ba9eeede"},{"text":"courtiers who fawn on a master while they betray him","ref":"1849–1861, Thomas Babington Macaulay, chapter XXIII, in The History of England from the Accession of James the Second, volume (please specify |volume=I to V), London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[14,18]],"id":"24c74ea5-fc71-4392-9467-2f99a9881e33"},{"text":"That the young Mr. Churchills liked—but they did not like him coming round of an evening and drinking weak whisky-and-water while he held forth on railway debentures and corporation loans. Mr. Barrett, however, by fawning and flattery, seemed to be able to make not only Mrs. Churchill but everyone else do what he desired.","ref":"1918, W[illiam] B[abington] Maxwell, chapter II, in The Mirror and the Lamp, Indianapolis, Ind.: The Bobbs-Merrill Company, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[214,221]],"id":"e612c29b-ca83-4299-8427-5f2ed1f809aa"}],"raw_glosses":["(figurative, by extension)","(derogatory) To seek favour by flattery and obsequious behaviour. [with on or upon]"],"info_templates":[{"args":{"1":"en","2":":on/:upon"},"name":"+obj","extra_data":{"words":["on","upon"]},"expansion":"[with on or upon]"}],"categories":["English derogatory terms","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["derogatory","derogatory"],["favour","favour"],["flattery","flattery"],["obsequious","obsequious"],["behaviour","behaviour"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"grovel"},{"word":"wheedle"},{"word":"soft-soap"},{"word":"toady"},{"word":"apple-polish","source":"Thesaurus:suck up","tags":["US","idiomatic"]},{"word":"bootlick","source":"Thesaurus:suck up"},{"word":"bow and scrape","source":"Thesaurus:suck up"},{"word":"brownnose","source":"Thesaurus:suck up"},{"word":"butter up","source":"Thesaurus:suck up"},{"